# /etc/eje/eje.env  — the real file on the VPS. NEVER commit the real one.
#
#   sudo install -d -m 0750 -o root -g eje /etc/eje
#   sudo install -m 0640 -o root -g eje /dev/null /etc/eje/eje.env
#
# Owned by root, readable by the eje group, and nothing else on the machine.

# WHICH DEPLOYMENT IS THIS? SAY SO. October mandate, Workstream A.
#
#   production      — real customers, real documents, real signatures.
#   demonstration   — EJE trying the application out. Fixtures expected.
#
# This is the line that lets every other guard stop guessing. `NODE_ENV` says
# `production` on BOTH deployments, because that is what makes Next serve a
# production build; the database name says production on the demonstration site
# today, because somebody named it that before the distinction mattered. So the
# deployment declares itself, and:
#
#   - a `production` deployment can NEVER arm EJE_DEMO_SWITCHER, whatever else
#     this file says. That refusal is in the runtime, not in a check;
#   - `npm run db:seed:demo` refuses a `production` deployment outright, with
#     no override — not even the named-database door below;
#   - a deployment pointed at a database whose name CONTRADICTS its declaration
#     fails preflight, which is what catches this file being copied from one
#     deployment to the other.
#
# Leave it unset and nothing is weakened: every guard behaves exactly as it did
# before this existed, and the preflight says the deployment is undeclared.
#
# A LINE IN A FILE IS NOT AN AIRLOCK. Separate credentials and separate
# databases are the real separation; this is the third line of defence. See
# src/ops/deployment-mode.ts.
EJE_DEPLOYMENT=production

# The database is on THIS machine. localhost, not a tunnel, not a public host.
#
# ONE DATABASE PER DEPLOYMENT, and they must not share a name. A demonstration
# deployment uses its own — `eje_demo` — so that no label, flag or screen is
# the only thing between EJE's real register and fictional customers:
#
#   production      DATABASE_URL=...@localhost:5432/eje_production
#   demonstration   DATABASE_URL=...@localhost:5432/eje_demo
#
# `eje_demo` rather than `eje_demonstration` on purpose: the connection guard's
# development-name list already recognises `demo`, so a development process
# that reaches it is permitted rather than refused for an unrevealing name.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://eje_app:REPLACE_ME@localhost:5432/eje_production

# Where job attachments and issued documents are written. On a volume that is
# actually backed up — see docs/integrations.md.
EJE_STORAGE_DIR=/var/lib/eje/storage

# SAID OUT LOUD RATHER THAN INFERRED.
#
# src/data/backend.ts falls back to the in-memory demonstration store when
# DATABASE_URL is absent or empty. That default is right for a sales laptop and
# wrong here: a typo in the line above would not fail, it would quietly serve a
# fictional company to real users with every write discarded on restart. Naming
# the backend turns that into a refusal — the connection is attempted and says
# DATABASE_URL is not set — instead of a silent fallback, and /api/health reports
# which backend is actually in use.
EJE_PERSISTENCE=postgres

# E-MAIL, through Microsoft 365 (Graph). MANDATE-3.
#
# THESE FOUR TOGETHER OR NOTHING. With all four set, the application sends
# through the tenant's own mailbox; with any of them missing it keeps the
# message in the visible outbox and SAYS SO on the notifications screen rather
# than pretending it went. There is no half-configured state that sends.
#
# The app registration needs the APPLICATION permission `Mail.Send` with admin
# consent granted, and EMAIL_SENDER must be a real licensed mailbox the
# registration may send as. `npm run integrations:check` reads the configuration
# without sending; `-- --probe` reads the sender's own mailbox to prove the
# consent is actually in place. See docs/integrations.md.
#
# THE SECRET BELONGS IN THIS FILE AND NOWHERE ELSE — not in the repository, not
# in a commit, not in a terminal that logs. The file is root-owned, mode 0600.
# EMAIL_GRAPH_TENANT_ID=
# EMAIL_GRAPH_CLIENT_ID=
# EMAIL_GRAPH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# EMAIL_SENDER=service@eje.co.za
#
# Optional. Replies go to EMAIL_SENDER unless this says otherwise, and the two
# URLs exist only so a test or a sovereign cloud can point elsewhere.
# EMAIL_REPLY_TO=
# EMAIL_GRAPH_BASE_URL=https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0
# EMAIL_GRAPH_LOGIN_URL=https://login.microsoftonline.com
# EMAIL_GRAPH_TIMEOUT_MS=20000

# READING THE TEXT A PDF ALREADY CONTAINS. LIB-TEXT-1.
#
# THIS IS THE LIBRARY'S PRIMARY SEARCH PATH, and it is the one dependency on
# this list that is NOT optional in practice. EJE's library holds manufacturer
# manuals, and `pdftotext` is what reads them: the built-in fallback reader
# cannot resolve a subset font's /ToUnicode map, which is how nearly every
# manual a machine builder ships is encoded. Without poppler installed the
# application still runs and is still honest — it reports those manuals as
# scans needing text recognition — but the search is far less useful than EJE
# expect, and `npm run preflight` WARNS about exactly that.
#
#   sudo apt-get install -y poppler-utils
#
# No variable needs setting: the better engine is the default when it is there.
# Nothing leaves the premises — `pdftotext` is a local process.
#
# EJE_PDF_TEXT=auto        # auto (default) | poppler | builtin
# EJE_PDF_TEXT_BINARY=/usr/bin/pdftotext
#
# `builtin` forces the fallback reader on a machine where poppler is present
# but unwanted. A misspelt value FAILS preflight rather than silently changing
# which engine reads EJE's manuals.

# TEXT RECOGNITION FOR SCANNED LIBRARY DOCUMENTS. LIB-5.
#
# The FALLBACK, for the pages that are genuinely pictures. Since LIB-TEXT-1,
# recognition is aimed only at the pages that carry no text — a 214-page manual
# with one scanned diagram is about 25 seconds of work rather than 83 minutes
# of it (BD-21's measurement).
#
# Unset on every deployment today, and that is a truthful state rather than a
# broken one: a document whose text cannot be lifted is recorded as NOT
# searchable, with the reason, and the queued recognition job settles as
# `unavailable` naming the packages that are missing. Nothing is sent anywhere.
#
# `local` is the ONLY value this build knows. It runs `pdftoppm` and `tesseract`
# as local processes — nothing leaves the premises — and needs:
#
#   sudo apt-get install -y tesseract-ocr poppler-utils
#
# then `npm run library:ocr -- --report`, which prints the engine, the limits
# and what is waiting. A misspelt value is reported as a misspelt value, not as
# an absent one. See docs/library-ocr.md, whose §3 decision (whether to install
# an engine at all) is EJE's and is still open.
# EJE_OCR=local
#
# EVERY DEPENDENCY AND EVERY LIMIT IS OVERRIDABLE, and all of them are optional.
# The full table is in docs/library-ocr.md; the two worth knowing about here are
# the whole-document DEADLINE — forty pages at the per-page timeout is six and a
# half hours on the machine that also serves the application — and the paths, for
# a tesseract that is installed somewhere this build does not look.
# EJE_OCR_TESSERACT=/usr/bin/tesseract
# EJE_OCR_PDFTOPPM=/usr/bin/pdftoppm
# EJE_OCR_LANGUAGE=eng
# EJE_OCR_MAX_PAGES=40
# EJE_OCR_DEADLINE_MINUTES=20
# EJE_OCR_MAX_INPUT_MB=40
# EJE_OCR_MAX_TEXT_MB=2

# SPREADSHEET EXPORTS ARE AUDITED. BD-18, ANSWERED — NOTHING TO SET.
#
# The owner answered BD-18 on 23 October 2026: audit all exports, and retain the
# records indefinitely. There is no longer a variable for this, and
# `EJE_EXPORT_AUDIT` is NO LONGER READ — if an earlier release's file sets it,
# the preflight says so and it can be deleted.
#
# WHAT IS RECORDED on every export: who took it and in which role, when, which
# export, the filters, the row count and the columns. NEVER THE ROWS — a trail
# says what happened rather than keeping the goods — and never the figures,
# even now that the office may export prices.
#
# RETENTION IS "INDEFINITELY" BY BEHAVIOUR, NOT BY PROMISE: nothing in this
# system expires or deletes an audit event. There is no retention job, no
# sweeper and no TTL, and the nightly backup carries these rows with every
# other. If EJE ever want a retention period, that is a new decision and a new
# piece of work.
#
# WHO MAY READ THEM: the office. The activity screen requires
# `activity.viewAll`, which a Master and a Coordinator hold and a technician
# does not. Exports appear there under their own "Exports" filter, kept separate
# from the record CHANGES that are every other event on the trail.

# WHICH BUILD THIS IS, for /api/health.
#
# Optional. Set it at BUILD time, not here: `next.config.ts` falls back to
# `git rev-parse --short HEAD`, which is correct when the release directory is a
# checkout and wrong when it is a copied tree. Setting it is how a deployment
# can be identified with certainty.
# EJE_BUILD_COMMIT=

# WhatsApp Business Platform. Leave BOTH unset and the application REFUSES to
# send rather than pretending: docs/integrations.md.
# WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
# WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# Optional, and only for `npm run integrations:check -- --probe`: the WhatsApp
# Business Account id, which is a DIFFERENT identifier from the phone number id
# and is not needed to send. With it, the check can read whether Meta holds
# APPROVED templates under the names this build sends (`eje_job_assigned`,
# `eje_site_location`). Without it, template approval is reported as unchecked
# rather than guessed either way.
# WHATSAPP_BUSINESS_ACCOUNT_ID=

# ONE-CLICK SIGN-IN AS THE PUBLISHED DEMONSTRATION ACCOUNTS. TEST DEPLOYMENTS ONLY.
#
# Uncomment ONLY on a demonstration deployment, and only where the value equals
# the database in DATABASE_URL above. A word meaning yes does nothing; the name
# is the mechanism, so this same line on a machine serving a differently-named
# database turns nothing on.
#
# It signs in as the five seeded accounts whose password is published in
# docs/development-seed.md, verifying that password against the stored hash — so
# a database holding real people offers it nothing even if this is set by
# mistake. That is the backstop, not the permission: NEVER set this on the live
# EJE deployment.
#
# GET /api/health reports "demoSwitcher": true wherever it is on.
#
# AND IT IS REFUSED OUTRIGHT where EJE_DEPLOYMENT=production, so on the live
# deployment this line cannot do anything at all. Leave it out of that file
# anyway: the next deployment to inherit the file may not declare itself.
# EJE_DEMO_SWITCHER=eje_demo

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BACKUPS. BACKUP-1, BACKUP-2. Read by `npm run backup` and its timers.
#
# The backup is ONE artefact: the database dump AND the documents under
# EJE_STORAGE_DIR, encrypted together. A dump on its own is a backup of the
# index — the rows point at signed job cards that would be gone.

# Where the backups are written. Must NOT be on the volume being backed up,
# and must not be inside EJE_STORAGE_DIR.
# EJE_BACKUP_DIR=/var/backups/eje

# 32 bytes as 64 hex characters: `openssl rand -hex 32`.
#
# KEEP A COPY SOMEWHERE THAT IS NOT THIS SERVER. A backup whose key is lost
# cannot be restored, and a key stored beside the backups protects nobody. It is
# refused rather than stretched if it is the wrong length, because a backup
# encrypted with something guessable would still report success.
# EJE_BACKUP_KEY=

# How many backups of THIS database to keep. Unset, or anything that is not a
# whole number above zero, keeps every backup — the failure mode of a misread
# retention setting must be a full disk, never a deleted backup. Nothing this
# system did not write is ever a candidate for removal.
# EJE_BACKUP_KEEP=14

# OFF THIS MACHINE. Pick one, or both, or neither and know that you have.
#
# A backup in /var/backups/eje is on the same machine as the documents it
# protects: it survives a mistaken DELETE and nothing else. Every run says so
# out loud when neither of these is set.
#
#   A mounted volume — an NFS share, an object-store mount, a disk that is
#   taken away. The run warns if it turns out to be the same filesystem as the
#   backups, because then it is a second copy on one disk.
# EJE_BACKUP_OFFSITE_DIR=/mnt/offsite/eje
#
#   An rclone remote, as `remote:path`. rclone holds its own configuration and
#   its own credentials, which this repository never sees and this file must
#   never carry: a cloud key here would be one `cat` away from every backup EJE
#   has. The run does `rclone copy` and then `rclone check`, which compares both
#   ends.
# EJE_BACKUP_OFFSITE_RCLONE=eje-offsite:eje-backups

# The restore target is deliberately a NEWER PostgreSQL than this server.
#
# `npm run backup` refuses when pg_dump is newer than the server it is dumping,
# because the dump cannot then be restored onto that server — see
# docs/vps-backups.md §4a. This says "I know, and the restore target is a newer
# server". Prefer installing the matching `postgresql-client-<major>`.
# EJE_BACKUP_ALLOW_VERSION_SKEW=yes

# A fresh deployment with nothing stored yet. Says "yes, there really are no
# documents"; on a deployment that has issued job cards, an empty storage
# directory means they are already gone and the backup is refused.
# EJE_BACKUP_ALLOW_EMPTY=yes

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THERE IS NO SESSION SECRET, AND THAT IS NOT AN OMISSION.
#
# Sessions are 256 random bits from the platform CSPRNG, stored as a SHA-256
# digest in the `sessions` table and handed to the browser in a `__Host-`
# cookie — src/server/auth/tokens.ts and sessions.ts. Nothing is signed, so
# there is no signing key to configure, rotate, or leave at a default. The
# passwords are Argon2id, whose salt lives in the hash. Every environment
# variable the application reads in production is above this line.
#
# WHAT MUST NOT GO IN THIS FILE.
#
#   EJE_PRODUCTION_MIGRATION   The gate in front of `npm run db:migrate` against
#                              the live schema. Putting it here would make it
#                              ambient — sourced by anything that sources this
#                              file — which is the entire thing it exists to
#                              prevent. It is typed on the command line, once,
#                              per migration. See docs/vps-deployment.md.
#   EJE_DATABASE_ALLOW         A development override. Meaningless here, because
#                              NODE_ENV=production already satisfies the guard.
#   EJE_SEED_ALLOW             The development seed does not run on this machine
#                              and cannot be made to.
